course: dictionary
lesson: 7799
title: Quarrelsome: ready to argue
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A quarrel is an angry argument, so quarrelsome describes someone who often argues or is quick to start one. Read the words around it to find the clue: «a quarrelsome neighbour» is a neighbour who is always arguing with people.
explain: Ask what word would fit. Try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word: «a neighbour who is always arguing» still makes sense, so you are right. Quarrelsome is about being ready to fight with others, not about being loud or sad.
explain: Look at the word's parts as a clue. Quarrel is the root, and -some often means "tending to" or "full of", so quarrelsome means tending to quarrel. Words in a phrase help too: «in a quarrelsome mood» tells you the person is ready to argue.
fact: Quarrelsome means often arguing or ready to start a quarrel.
fact: A quarrel is an angry argument between people.
fact: «A quarrelsome child» is someone who is easily angry and likes to argue with others.
fact: In the Greek myths, the gods on Mount Olympus «behaved a lot like quarrelsome humans», so they argued and fell out like people do.
fact: Quarrelsome and grumpy are close in meaning, but quarrelsome shows someone who picks fights with others.
exercise: «A quarrelsome neighbour» is a neighbour who is always doing what? | Giving away flowers | Singing in the garden | Sleeping late every day | *Arguing with people
exercise: In the Greek sentence, which gods are said to behave like quarrelsome humans? | The gods who lived under the sea | *The gods living on Mount Olympus | Nobody, because the gods were always calm | The wild god Pan only
exercise: Nadia says quarrelsome is just another word for grumpy. Why might an author choose quarrelsome? | *It shows someone who starts arguments, not just someone who is grumpy | It means happy and friendly | It means exactly the same as grumpy, so there is no reason to pick it | It is a shorter word
test: «A quarrelsome child» means what? | Happy to share and take turns | *Easily angry and likely to argue with others | Tired and sleepy at bedtime
test: Which phrase best matches «in a quarrelsome mood»? | *Ready to argue about anything | Full of laughter at the party | Calm and ready for a nap
exam: Which sentence uses quarrelsome correctly? | The quarrelsome book was read quietly by the fire | The quarrelsome baby drifted off to sleep | *The quarrelsome twins kept shouting about whose turn it was | The quarrelsome sunrise painted the sky gold
